{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016WixMP7eJxLRXKuW34Bq5x","type":"tool_result","content":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-assign-pod-node-2026-08-23.md:13:Like many other Kubernetes objects, nodes have labels. You can attach labels manually. Kubernetes also populates a standard set of labels on all nodes in a cluster. Adding labels to nodes allows you to target Pods for scheduling on specific nodes or groups of nodes; if you use labels for node isolation, choose label keys that the kubelet cannot modify (the NodeRestriction admission plugin prevents the kubelet from setting labels with a node-restriction.kubernetes.io/ prefix).\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-cluster-administration-2026-08-23.md:6:concepts_covered: [\"planning-a-cluster\", \"managed-vs-self-hosted\", \"node-management\", \"resource-quota\", \"certificates\", \"authentication\", \"authorization\", \"admission-controllers\", \"auditing\", \"tls-bootstrapping\"]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-cluster-administration-2026-08-23.md:17:Generate Certificates; Kubernetes Container Environment; Controlling Access to the Kubernetes API; Authenticating; Authorization; Using Admission Controllers; Admission Webhook Good Practices; Using Sysctls in a Kubernetes Cluster; Auditing.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23.md:6:concepts_covered: [\"extension-points\", \"cri\", \"cni\", \"csi\", \"device-plugins\", \"webhooks\", \"admission-control\", \"crd\", \"api-aggregation\", \"scheduler-extensions\"]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23.md:19:2. API access extensions — authentication, authorization, dynamic admission control (webhooks).\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-pod-qos-2026-08-24.md:60:* The QoS class is determined when the Pod is created and remains unchanged for the lifetime of the Pod. If you later attempt an in-place resize that would result in a different QoS class, the resize is rejected by admission.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-pod-security-standards-2026-08-23.md:6:concepts_covered: [\"pod-security-standards\", \"privileged\", \"baseline\", \"restricted\", \"pod-security-admission\", \"namespace-labels\"]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-pod-security-standards-2026-08-23.md:24:The standards are enforced by the built-in Pod Security Admission controller, which applies a policy per namespace via labels of the form pod-security.kubernetes.io/<MODE>: <LEVEL>, where MODE is enforce (violations are rejected), audit (violations are recorded in the audit log), or warn (violations trigger a user-facing warning), and LEVEL is privileged, baseline, or restricted.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-taints-tolerations-depth-2026-08-24.md:39:> custom admission controller). The pods with the tolerations will then be allowed to use\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-taints-tolerations-depth-2026-08-24.md:47:> nodes (e.g. `dedicated=groupName`), and the admission controller should additionally\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-well-known-labels-2026-08-24.md:50:`k8s-docs-assign-pod-node-2026-08-23.md`: \"the NodeRestriction admission plugin prevents\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\kyverno-overview-2026-08-23.md:6:concepts_covered: [\"kyverno\", \"policy-engine\", \"admission-controller\", \"validate\", \"mutate\", \"generate\", \"image-verification\", \"opa-gatekeeper\"]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\kyverno-overview-2026-08-23.md:10:[Omitted long matching line]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\sigstore-overview-2026-08-23.md:12:Components: **Cosign** — the client tool for signing and verifying software artifacts, including container images; **Fulcio** — the code-signing certificate authority that issues short-lived certificates bound to a verified identity rather than long-lived keys; **Rekor** — an immutable, append-only transparency log that records signing events for public audit and verification; **Policy Controller** — enforces signature verification policies within Kubernetes as an admission controller."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fff25e8c-a241-4a0c-a9a8-91a6171ab2de","uuid":"f72da037-29bc-4130-8628-bd74b5a6c7b1","timestamp":"2026-08-24T23:26:56.300Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-assign-pod-node-2026-08-23.md:13:Like many other Kubernetes objects, nodes have labels. You can attach labels manually. Kubernetes also populates a standard set of labels on all nodes in a cluster. Adding labels to nodes allows you to target Pods for scheduling on specific nodes or groups of nodes; if you use labels for node isolation, choose label keys that the kubelet cannot modify (the NodeRestriction admission plugin prevents the kubelet from setting labels with a node-restriction.kubernetes.io/ prefix).\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-cluster-administration-2026-08-23.md:6:concepts_covered: [\"planning-a-cluster\", \"managed-vs-self-hosted\", \"node-management\", \"resource-quota\", \"certificates\", \"authentication\", \"authorization\", \"admission-controllers\", \"auditing\", \"tls-bootstrapping\"]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-cluster-administration-2026-08-23.md:17:Generate Certificates; Kubernetes Container Environment; Controlling Access to the Kubernetes API; Authenticating; Authorization; Using Admission Controllers; Admission Webhook Good Practices; Using Sysctls in a Kubernetes Cluster; Auditing.\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23.md:6:concepts_covered: [\"extension-points\", \"cri\", \"cni\", \"csi\", \"device-plugins\", \"webhooks\", \"admission-control\", \"crd\", \"api-aggregation\", \"scheduler-extensions\"]\nC:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\k8s