s the **namespace in aggregate**. The other mechanism is **LimitRange**, which constrains **individual objects** and supplies defaults so that Pods specify their resource requirements at all.\n418:## 🔵 §4 — Taking a Node Out of Service\n422:### Where nodes come from\n430:### The three commands\n438:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig04-node-lifecycle-cordon-drain -->\n461:### Node conditions\n483:### Heartbeats, and a control loop you already know\n493:### Capacity and Allocatable\n499:[Omitted long matching line]\n505:## ⚪ §5 — Who Owns the Control Plane\n511:### The planning questions\n523:### The tools, split by what they are for\n531:### One requirement none of these removes\n539:### What ownership actually means\n555:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2 — The Machine, and Whose It Is\n559:**1.** 🔵 **[retrieval: ch7]** Chapter 7 listed `node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable` with a `NoSchedule` effect among the built-in condition taints, and noted that this one is applied deliberately rather than by a failing component. What command applies it — and, using Chapter 7's rule about what `NoSchedule` governs, what happens to the Pods already running on that node?\n561:**2.** 🟡 **Challenge item — this one is meant to be uncomfortable, and the intuition it breaks is a reasonable intuition.** An engineer cordons a node and immediately reboots it for maintenance. Three services go down. What did they skip, and why did the cordon not prevent it?\n563:**3.** 🔵 A node stops responding. Its `Ready` condition changes. To what value — and what does that value mean that `False` would not?\n565:**4.** 🔵 **[retrieval: ch2]** You bootstrap a cluster with kubeadm. Before any node in it can run a Pod, one piece of software must already be installed on that node which kubeadm does not provide. Name it, name the two implementations the documentation names, and say which interface Kubernetes uses to talk to it.\n567:**5.** 🔵 Two engineers describe their clusters. One says \"we upgraded to 1.36 last Tuesday.\" The other says \"we *got* upgraded to 1.36 last Tuesday.\" What is different about their situations, and name one further duty that differs between them.\n572:**1.** `kubectl cordon`. **Nothing happens to the running Pods.** `NoSchedule` governs new placements only, so Pods already on the node keep running.\n576:**2.** They skipped **`kubectl drain`**. `cordon` stops new Pods arriving and deliberately leaves running Pods alone; the three services were still on that node when the machine went down.\n580:**3.** **`Unknown`.** The node controller has not heard from the node within the `node-monitor-grace-period`. `False` would mean something different and more specific: the node reported *itself* unhealthy and not accepting Pods. `Unknown` is the control plane saying it has no current information, which is consistent with a dead machine and equally consistent with a network partition.\n584:**4.** A **container runtime** — **containerd** or **CRI-O** — and Kubernetes talks to it through the **CRI**, the Container Runtime Interface.\n588:**5.** The first engineer owns their control plane (self-hosted); the second is on a managed platform where the provider holds the upgrade calendar. Further duties that differ: **etcd backup** (the standard second answer), certificate rotation, control-plane machine maintenance, and control-plane component configuration such as scheduler profiles.\n610:## 🟡 §6 — Versions That Are Allowed to Disagree\n620:### The vocabulary\n626:### The cadence, which makes the branch count make sense\n636:### The rules\n638:> **Dead Reckoning:** The supported version skew, stated flat.\n650:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-fig03-version-skew-window -->\n668:### The exception, which is where the exam points are\n682:### What the rule implies about upgrade order\n694:## 🔵 §7 — The One Backup That Matters\n706:### The mechanics\n712:<!-- AUTHOR-REVIEW: the TLS flags are named above because the backup snapshot lists them. No configuration guidance is given, deliberately: the etcd-access-control snapshot's note states that the source page's TLS configuration guidance was not verbatim-verified in that fetch. Do not expand this into how to configure etcd TLS without a fresh verified fetch. -->\n716:### The fact that matters more than the commands\n738:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3 — The Two Things You Cannot Improvise\n742:**1.** 🔵 Your cluster's API servers are at 1.36. For each of the following, say whether it is supported: (a) a kubelet at 1.33; (b) a kubelet at 1.37; (c) a `kubectl` at 1.37; (d) a kube-scheduler at 1.34.\n744:**2.** 🟡 State the one rule that generates four of the five rows of the skew table. Then name the row it does *not* generate, and say why that row is different.\n746:**3.** 🔵 How many minor releases does the project maintain release branches for? Roughly how long is patch support for 1.19 and newer? Roughly how many minor releases ship per year? Then say why those three numbers are consistent with each other.\n748:**4.** 🟡 You lose every control-plane node. Your worker nodes are untouched and your applications are still serving traffic. What have you actually lost — and what single artefact would let you get it back?\n750:**5.** 🔵 An etcd snapshot is sitting, unencrypted, on one of your control-plane nodes. Give two *separate* reasons this is wrong.\n755:**1.** (a) **Supported** — kubelet may be up to three minor versions older than kube-apiserver. (b) **Not supported** — kubelet must never be newer than kube-apiserver. (c) **Supported** — `kubectl` is permitted within one minor version in either direction, and this is the only component for which \"newer\" is allowed. (d) **Not supported** — kube-scheduler may be at most one minor version older than the API servers it talks to, and 1.34 is two behind 1.36.\n759:**2.** The rule: **nothing may be newer than the API server.** It generates the kubelet row, the kube-proxy row, the controller-manager/scheduler/cloud-controller-manager row, and the constraint on HA API servers relative to one another.\n765:**3.** **Three** release branches. **Approximately one year** of patch support for 1.19 and newer. **Approximately three** minor releases per year, roughly every fifteen weeks.\n771:**4.** You have lost **the cluster's entire record of intent**, every object, since all Kubernetes objects are stored in etcd. Not the running workloads: kubelets keep running what they were last told to run, and traffic keeps flowing. What is gone is every declaration of what *should* be running, which means nothing can be reconciled, changed, scheduled, healed or scaled.\n777:**5.** **First:** it is not stored outside the nodes it protects against losing. The documented instruction is to store it outside the control plane nodes, precisely because a snapshot that lives only on the machines whose loss it insures against does not survive the event it exists for.\n800:## ⚪ §8 — Rules, or Consequences\n824:<!-- FIGURE: ch08-zenith-consequences-not-rules -->\n842:### Rules, or consequences","numLines":90,"totalLines":110,"appliedLimit":90}}
